title: Calculate Grid
keyword: CUBE
manual: graphics/#c2-cube

Calculates the values of a field of the system, one of its derivatives, an arithmetic
expression of the fields or a wavefunction on a three-dimensional grid, and writes them to a
cube, XSF or VASP file, or loads them as a new field of the system. Use it to export a
field to another program or to turn an expensive field into a grid field.

# Options

- "Property": a quantity of a field (value, gradient components, gradient norm, Hessian
  components or Laplacian) or an "Expression" of the fields.
- "Wavefunction" (grid fields from a Quantum ESPRESSO pwc file): the field itself or one of
  the wavefunctions in the file (a Bloch function, its periodic part, a Wannier function or a
  maximally localized Wannier function), with its "Band", "k-point", "Spin", "Lattice
  translation" and the "Part" written (absolute value, real or imaginary part). A
  wavefunction is written on the nodes of its own grid.
- "Region": the "Mode" of the region (the whole cell, a box, a parallelepiped, the native
  grid of a grid field or, in molecules, the box bounding the atoms grown by a buffer), with
  the coordinates of its points and a "Pick" button for each.
- "Grid": "Given as" the number of "Points" along each side or the "Spacing (Å)" between
  neighboring points. For the native grid (More): a "Shift" of the nodes and a "Supercell"
  repeating the grid along the lattice vectors. "Estimate Cost" predicts the time the grid
  takes. "Header only" (More) writes only the cell and the atoms.

# Output

"Write to File" writes the grid: a cube file for any region, or an XSF or VASP file for the
whole cell. "Load as Field" loads it as a new field of the system instead (not with "Header
only" or a supercell; in a crystal, only on the unit cell or the native grid).

# Notes

The region is shown as a box in the view while the window is open. Very large grids take
long to calculate and write: check the cost estimate first.
